WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.437
- So I'm really excited to welcome

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:11.780
Dr. Joseph Scalice here today.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.380
Dr. Scalice is currently a visiting fellow

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.978
at the Saw Swee Hock Southeast Asia Center

00:00:16.978 --> 00:00:20.700
at the London School of
Economics and Political Science.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.467
And he specializes in the history

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:26.037
of revolutionary movements
and authoritarianism

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:31.037
in Southeast Asia with a focus
on the post-war Philippines.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.240
He's a very prolific scholar.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.380
Since completing his PhD
in Southeast Asian studies

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.640
at UC Berkeley, my Alma Mater,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.021
by the way, where I did my BA,

00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:42.860
at where he completed his PhD there

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.472
in 2018 at UC Berkeley, he has published

00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:48.828
no fewer than eight peer
reviewed journal articles,

00:00:48.828 --> 00:00:51.820
including, but not all of them,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.909
I'll say, just of the most recent ones.

00:00:53.909 --> 00:00:56.338
In 2021, I guess, forthcoming still,

00:00:56.338 --> 00:01:01.338
A Deliberately Forgotten
Battle: The Lapiang Manggagawa

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.840
and the Manila Port Strike of 1963 in the,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.150
coming out in the Journal
of Southeast Asian studies.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.303
2021, again, Cadre as Informal Diplomats:

00:01:12.303 --> 00:01:17.303
Ferdinand Marcos in the Soviet
Bloc, 1965 through 1974.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.380
That's in History and Anthropology.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.400
And also in 2021, this year,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.280
The Geopolitical Alignments
of Diverging Social Interests.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.200
The Sino-Soviet Split in the
Partido Komunista ng Pilipinas,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.200
1966 to '67 in Critical Asian Studies.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.210
He also has a book manuscript

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.140
under contract with
Cornell University Press.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.690
It's called, "The Drama of
Dictatorship: Martial Law

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.880
and the Communist Parties
of the Philippines,"

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.480
which should be coming out
probably in a year or so.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.670
And a whole slew of book
chapters, published reviews,

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.850
and a lot of wonderful things for everyone

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.033
to look up and start reading immediately.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.260
I won't say more because we're going

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.430
to learn a lot from the talk,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.386
and hopefully we'll have
a great Q&amp;A after this,

00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:09.390
but please join me in
welcoming Dr. Joseph Scalice

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.730
to our Virtual Brown Bag series.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.020
His talk today is entitled:

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.300
The Geopolitical Alignments
of Diverging Social Interest,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.330
the Sino-Soviet Split in the Philippines.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.500
Please take it away, Joseph.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.240
- Thank you very much, Eric,
I appreciate the introduction.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.517
I appreciate the opportunity
to address the audience

00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:32.410
on a topic that I think
is really exciting,

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.390
which is to say the Sino-Soviet
Split in the periphery,

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:38.900
not in the core disputants of
the Soviet Union and China,

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.630
but in, throughout the
global south in particular,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.856
and the Philippines as a case study.

00:02:44.856 --> 00:02:49.739
The listeners may have noted
that the topic of the talk,

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:52.910
The Geopolitical Alignments
of Diverging Social Interests

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.820
corresponds with the title
of an article published

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.260
earlier this year in
Critical Asian Studies.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.730
I'm expanding upon that article towards

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.220
what is a second book proposal
that I've recently submitted.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.200
I have a forthcoming book
with Cornell University Press,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.600
which I hope will be out next year,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.330
on the subject of martial
law and the role played

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.830
by two rival communist parties,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.640
which I've entitled, "The
Drama of Dictatorship."

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.867
It's an extraordinarily
heated and exciting

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:24.540
and terrifying development
from 1969 to 1972,

00:03:25.744 --> 00:03:28.170
as all of the various factions

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.753
of the ruling elite are angling for,

00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:35.190
in one in one way or
another, authoritarian rule.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.010
And two rival parties, two
rival communist parties,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.710
tied to Moscow and Beijing,
played a critical role in this,

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.920
and that's what my book documents.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.490
But it raises inevitably a question,

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.473
why were there two communist
parties, and not one?

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.430
And why were they, what
were their allegiances

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.810
to this dispute between
China and the Soviet union?

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.120
And that is what I'm attempting

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.100
to explore in this talk,
and in my second book,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.140
which I have submitted
the book proposal for,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.410
which I've entitled, "Aligned Rivalries:

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.980
The Sino-Soviet Split From the Periphery."

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.494
And the question that occupies me is,

00:04:13.494 --> 00:04:18.494
how was it that the political
rivalries in the Philippines,

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:20.790
and throughout the global south,

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.750
throughout what I'm calling
the periphery to this dispute,

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:27.885
how was it that they
aligned with the interests

00:04:27.885 --> 00:04:30.943
of Moscow and Beijing, of
the Soviet Union and China?

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.300
And I'm trying to avoid narratives

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.850
that either implied
that it was the product

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.720
of external machinations effectively,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.000
the argument of the right,
that these were agents

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.900
of the Soviet union or
China, or what I find

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.872
to be an equally, say a
dead end, scholarly, is

00:04:48.872 --> 00:04:51.080
that these were the product
of domestic disputes

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.805
and really had nothing to do
with international communism,

00:04:53.805 --> 00:04:56.370
that as I will demonstrate in a moment.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.343
That argument simply does not hold up.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.788
So that then is the origins of this talk.

00:05:02.788 --> 00:05:05.600
And I'm going to be examining the role

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.494
of the Philippine Communist
Party, and why it was

00:05:07.494 --> 00:05:11.370
that it split along the lines
of the Sino-Soviet dispute.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.577
There's been very little
written upon this topic.

00:05:14.577 --> 00:05:18.290
The question of why in the 1960s,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.050
in 1966 to '67, in particular,

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.323
the Philippine Communist Party,

00:05:22.323 --> 00:05:26.130
Partido Komunista ng
Pilipinas, split in two,

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.530
and out of this emerged
the new communist party,

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.720
the Communist Party of
the Philippines, CPP.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.020
And so while the names are
simply Tagalog and English,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.560
and mean the same thing, for our purposes,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.710
in this talk, the PKP, the Tagalog name,

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.593
the older party, is the one
that allied with Moscow.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:51.138
And the CPP, which still
exists to this day,

00:05:51.138 --> 00:05:53.410
and is a strong political influence

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:58.290
to this day, is the party
that was founded in 1968-69,

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:03.290
and allied with Beijing, with
China, so the PKP and the CPP.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.550
But throughout the
first half of the 1960s,

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:10.020
there was but one party, the PKP.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.620
And the question is, why
did it split in '66, '67?

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.000
What led to this?

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.240
And how do we account for the fact

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.430
that it corresponded to
the Sino-Soviet dispute

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.260
in a way that is
intellectually satisfying?

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:25.600
There has been very little
prior scholarship on this.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.290
The most influential explanation
was not even published.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.780
It's an unpublished paper
delivered, I believe in Canberra,

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:37.151
the ANU, by a man named Francisco
Nemenzo, Dodong Nemenzo,

00:06:37.151 --> 00:06:39.180
a very influential intellectual figure,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.880
a man who was himself a leading member

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.060
of the central committee
of the PKP at the time,

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.360
and went on to become President

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.950
of the University of the Philippines.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:51.143
And in 1984, he put
forward this explanation.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.870
The schism was not a local expression

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.240
of the international dispute

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.520
between the Soviet and Chinese parties,

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.370
but the offshoot of a generational rift

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.020
between the remnants of
an aborted rebellion,

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.500
I'll get to that in a moment,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.270
and the new elements who were
spared the trauma of defeat.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.500
So, on the one hand, these
remnants of the Huk Rebellion,

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.970
which I'll talk about in a sec,

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.810
and these newer layers, the youth,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.040
who came into the party
and were spared the trauma

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.600
of the defeat of the Huk Rebellion.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.990
That, he argues, is
the seeds of the split.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:27.120
But since it occurred at the
height of Sino-Soviet dispute,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.600
what was a domestic quarrel assumed

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.760
an international dimension.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.240
Neither the Communist
Party of the Soviet Union,

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.330
nor the Communist Party of
China were initially involved.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:43.470
Their interventions, which
came later, aggravated

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.430
rather than triggered the conflict.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:47.970
Now I see Nemenzo's
Intervention in 1984 as

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.990
something of a salutary contribution,

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.810
despite the fact that I
fundamentally disagree with it.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.420
He was pushing back against
the standard narrative

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.370
that, put forward by the right and agents

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:01.200
of the state and the Marcos dictatorship,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.255
this is still the reign of
Ferdinand Marcos in 1984,

00:08:05.255 --> 00:08:08.620
that the split and the allegiance

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.930
of the rival parties was a product

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.460
of external agents, external machinations,

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:17.580
that these were effectively
agents of foreign powers.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.700
And he's saying, no, this
was a domestic dispute.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.970
But it is not, I argue, convincing

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.410
or intellectually satisfying
on a number of levels.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:30.440
One, it cannot account for
the extraordinary simultaneity

00:08:31.316 --> 00:08:33.911
of this in which parties around the globe,

00:08:33.911 --> 00:08:37.300
in Latin America, in India, in New Zealand

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:40.220
and Australia, et
cetera, all of them split

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.360
within years of each other
along the same lines.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.470
There is a sort of nationalist
myopia to this argument

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.080
that sees this as a domestic dispute.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.140
And so while the pushback
against the argument

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:54.450
of agents is a healthy
one, the alternative

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.016
that is posited, a domestic dispute

00:08:56.016 --> 00:09:00.820
that took on international
coloration, is unsatisfying.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.859
What is more, the idea
of a generational rift,

00:09:03.859 --> 00:09:06.620
does not hold up to scrutiny.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.830
Many of the older layers who had been tied

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.020
to a peasant rebellion in the 1950s known

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.113
as the Huk Rebellion, which was defeated,

00:09:13.113 --> 00:09:16.070
many of these older layers
in fact gave their support

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:19.702
to the new CPP, allied to
the Chinese Communist party.

00:09:19.702 --> 00:09:24.442
And in the same fashion, the
majority of the party's youth,

00:09:24.442 --> 00:09:28.020
who did not go through
the sting of defeat,

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.740
remained tied to the PKP, the majority,

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.740
including Nemenzo himself,

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:34.490
who was a man of the same youth

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:37.323
as those who broke from
the party to found the CPP.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:41.264
The argument doesn't hold
up on a number of levels.

00:09:41.264 --> 00:09:43.934
Finally, there is the claim
that the dispute was simply one

00:09:43.934 --> 00:09:47.650
over violence, this is
not a scholarly argument

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:49.210
so much as a common conception.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:52.093
The CPP advocated armed
struggle, the PKP opposed it,

00:09:52.093 --> 00:09:55.350
this again is fundamentally wrong.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.580
The PKP had an armed wing just as much

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.360
as the CPP did, and the
armed wing carried out

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.050
guerrilla struggle and bombings.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:05.490
This was not the divide, what was?

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.150
What accounts for this
alignment of interests

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:12.910
between the Soviet Union and
the PKP, the CPP and China,

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.700
as well as with rival
factions of the elite?

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.420
And how do we account for the simultaneity

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.033
of the Sino-Soviet split?

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.550
I am inspired in this by something

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.900
that I was reading over dinner,

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:29.370
as you read other scholars
from other fields,

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.860
and I was reading
Braudel, and Braudel wrote

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.380
in his book, "Out of
Italy," of the dialectic

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.940
of the internal and external
of the Italian Renaissance.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:40.140
And he noted that it is sometimes said

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.510
that light shed from
the margin is the best,

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:45.754
that a complex whole
may best be apprehended

00:10:45.754 --> 00:10:48.616
from its outer limits, in a situation

00:10:48.616 --> 00:10:53.470
in which every fact, every
event has been minutely studied

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:56.160
by the generations of devoted historians,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.910
the vantage point of the periphery,

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:01.070
of the diaspora, can provide new clarity

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:02.470
to developments in the core.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.290
Now, I find this particularly useful

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.800
for examining the Sino-Soviet split.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:09.480
The development of global
Maoism, the development

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.040
of international Communism
from the vantage point

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.420
of the core disputants has
been meticulously analyzed.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.060
What I would argue has been seriously

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.505
under studied is that this was

00:11:23.505 --> 00:11:26.220
an integrated international phenomenon.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.780
And that the periphery, that is to say,

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.010
parties around the globe,
split along the lines

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:33.551
of the Sino-Soviet
dispute, not as some form

00:11:33.551 --> 00:11:37.810
of collateral damage, blow
back, an unintended consequence

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.437
of a dispute between two
national entities, but rather

00:11:40.437 --> 00:11:45.437
as a product of a coherent
integrated international whole.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.390
And that by viewing
the Sino-Soviet dispute

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.160
from the periphery, we
can gain new insight,

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.940
not only into the disputes
within the periphery itself,

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.820
but to the development of global Maoism,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:02.270
to the character of the global Cold War,

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:03.750
and to the nature of the dispute

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:08.750
between Moscow and Beijing in
the 1950s, 1960s and 1970s.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:18.528
At the core of my argument is the claim

00:12:18.528 --> 00:12:23.528
that Stalinism is, it's a
number of things, this term,

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:29.100
but among them is a
coherent political program.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:32.400
I think most commonly
in everyday parlance,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:36.090
we associate the word Stalinism correctly

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:39.650
with the brutal excesses
of the bureaucracy

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:44.650
under Stalin, and also under
Mao, responsible for purges,

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:50.800
for the Gulag, for the
revision and rewriting

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:52.800
of history, et cetera, all of the things

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.730
that we associate, I think,
with the name Orwell, et cetera.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:58.250
These things are true, but I argue

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:01.240
that they are necessary manifestations

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.890
of a core political program
and by examining this program,

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.750
we see that there is in fact,
a fundamental continuity

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.270
between Stalinism and Maoism,

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:13.510
and that Maoism, I would argue,
is not something separate

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:17.270
from, but is a variant of Stalinism

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:20.630
under particular social circumstances.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.900
What then is this political program?

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.410
Well, I break it down into
four very simple points.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.080
Socialism in one country,
a two stage theory

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.500
of revolution, and the
Bloc of four classes.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.690
These were the programmatic
nature of Stalinism.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:37.030
You can see here, the artwork
of the Communist party

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.480
of the Philippines produced in the 1970s

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.450
that shows the bloc of four classes.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:43.250
I'll get back to that in just a moment

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.080
to explain this illustration.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.420
Stalinism at its essence expressed

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.150
the political interest
of privileged layers

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.210
of the party bureaucracy
within the Soviet Union,

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.263
and after 1949, in China.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:56.700
Seeking to defend and
expand the social basis

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.240
of their positions, these
bureaucrats put forward

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.030
a nationalist program
of building socialism

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:05.200
in one country as the
paramount political task,

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:07.263
rather than world socialist revolution.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.771
Looking to secure diplomatic
and trade relations

00:14:10.771 --> 00:14:14.110
and service to the construction
of their national economies,

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:16.670
Stalinist bureaucrats
sought political capital

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.357
with which to negotiate
with a ruling class

00:14:18.357 --> 00:14:21.150
in countries around the world.

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:24.302
To this end, they rehabilitated
the old Menshevik line

00:14:24.302 --> 00:14:26.820
of a two-stage revolution.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.380
They instructed Communist
parties around the globe

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.360
that the tasks of the revolution

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.230
in which they were engaged
were not yet socialist

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:38.340
in character, but were
national and democratic only.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:39.860
A section of the capitalist class,

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:42.350
they argued, would play a progressive role

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:44.050
in this necessary first stage.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:47.710
So the tasks in this first
stage of a two-stage revolution,

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:49.288
the national democratic stage,

00:14:49.288 --> 00:14:52.490
still have a Capitalist character,

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:55.190
and therefore a section of
the capitalist class will play

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.890
a progressive role, that's the argument.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.090
The goal of Communist party
leaders should thus be

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.730
to secure an alliance with
this progressive section

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:05.570
of the national bourgeoisie,
and to bring the pressure

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:09.310
and support of a mass movement
behind their elite allies.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:11.420
Now, this is what we
see in the illustration.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:14.370
This is from a book on how to use drawing

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.423
to instruct the masses, and you can see,

00:15:16.423 --> 00:15:19.287
on the top layer, mga kaaway, the enemies.

00:15:19.287 --> 00:15:21.650
And the enemies are Uncle Sam,

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:24.000
the imperialist United States,

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:28.600
the Comprador Bourgeoisie,
the fat capitalist up top,

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:31.030
and the landlord, panginoong maylupa.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:34.023
But on the bottom, we see
the bloc of four classes.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:37.310
And this is comprised of
friends, mga kaibigan.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:40.310
manggagawa, the worker,
magsasaka, the peasant,

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:43.280
peti-burgesya, some of these
hardly require translation,

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.494
it's the petty bourgeoisie,
and pambansang burgesya,

00:15:46.494 --> 00:15:48.460
that is to say the national bourgeoisie.

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:50.690
These are the four progressive classes,

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:53.870
and notice that a section
of the capitalist class is,

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:58.400
by this definition, progressive,
an ally of the workers.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.380
A section of the bosses
is an ally of the workers,

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:05.540
that's the critical
point in this first stage

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.610
of a two-stage revolution.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.370
Stalinism was first and
foremost a political program

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:11.360
that articulated the interest

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.329
of the ruling party bureaucracies
before it was denounced

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:16.812
by Khrushchev in his '56 Secret Speech

00:16:16.812 --> 00:16:20.690
of show trials, purges and
the cult of the great leader.

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:22.260
At its essence, it was a program

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.200
before it was these other things.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.340
These were the mechanisms
routinely employed

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.500
by Stalinism to maintain
a bureaucratic hold

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:29.940
on power, but they were not the essence

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:32.030
of what was disseminated around the globe.

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:33.783
The global strength of Stalinism rested

00:16:33.783 --> 00:16:38.190
on the appeal to a layer of
nationalist intellectuals

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.300
in countries of belated
capitalist development

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.970
of its core concepts,
socialism in one country,

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:44.157
a two-stage theory of revolution,

00:16:44.157 --> 00:16:46.870
and the bloc of four progressive classes.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:48.270
Many communist party leaders outside

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.098
of the Soviet Union
were drawn to Stalinism

00:16:51.098 --> 00:16:53.070
because they saw this as a means

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:56.450
of implementing national reforms.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:17:01.060
It allowed them to deploy
the banner of Marxism

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:04.840
and use it to win mass
support for industrialization

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:07.110
under native capitalist ownership.

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.400
So the program of national reforms,

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:11.217
national industrialization,
the development

00:17:11.217 --> 00:17:15.740
of a sort of autarkic
national capitalist economy,

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:19.350
a very much at core concept
of nationalist intelligentsia

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:24.346
in the 1950s and '60s,
required mass enthusiasm

00:17:24.346 --> 00:17:28.083
to carry it out, but how
do you secure the support

00:17:28.083 --> 00:17:30.963
of workers and peasants for
what effectively is a series

00:17:30.963 --> 00:17:35.330
of protectionist measures on
behalf of national capitalism?

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:37.400
It amounts to trickle down economics.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:40.750
If we can improve national
capitalism, it will float,

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:43.460
rising tide floats all
boats, that sort of logic.

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:45.520
I mean, this is of course
long before Reagan,

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.671
but that's the argument, and the answer is

00:17:47.671 --> 00:17:50.450
that Stalinism provides.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:52.518
You can use the banner of Marxism,

00:17:52.518 --> 00:17:56.730
of revolution, et cetera,
to dress up the first stage

00:17:56.730 --> 00:18:00.010
of a necessary two-stage
revolution as progressive,

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:02.410
and then the alliance
with the capitalist class.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:05.550
Loans from and trade
with the Soviet bloc were

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:08.150
an additional measure and
furtherance to these ends.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:12.220
It was for this reason that the PKP,

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:14.300
as I'll detail in just a
moment, gave its support

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:17.870
to Ferdinand Marcos in
1965, proclaiming him

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:21.040
the progressive representative
of the national bourgeoisie.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.923
This is a year prior to the
split in the communist party.

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:27.508
There was a contradiction, however,

00:18:27.508 --> 00:18:30.410
at the heart of this Stalinist program.

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:34.680
That contradiction is that
it sought simultaneously

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:38.320
to retain hold over a mass movement,

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.880
and to secure an alliance with a section

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:43.040
of the elite, it sought to do both.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.440
It was inevitable that it had to do both.

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.830
Its alliance with a section
of the elite was predicated

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:49.440
upon its hold over a mass movement,

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:50.990
that was its political capital.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:55.770
Under situations of
social unrest, however,

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:58.910
this contradiction became
increasingly unstable,

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:00.880
hard to hold together.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:02.858
How do you retain hold
over a mass movement

00:19:02.858 --> 00:19:06.098
and an alliance with
the elite in a situation

00:19:06.098 --> 00:19:08.810
that is becoming
increasingly revolutionary,

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:13.240
increasingly explosive, and
in the context of the 1960s,

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.570
this contradiction at the heart

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:16.940
of Stalinism tore itself apart,

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:19.099
diverging social interests that found

00:19:19.099 --> 00:19:22.750
that the ideological form
of the national dispute

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.520
between the Soviet Union
and China gave expression

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.760
to these diverging social interests,

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:31.823
an alignment between this
program and its variance

00:19:31.823 --> 00:19:35.470
with diverging interests
at a national level.

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:36.736
This is what I'm going to establish

00:19:36.736 --> 00:19:39.863
in a sort of the case
study of the Philippines.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:45.443
Why, what were the variants
of the Soviet Union in China?

00:19:45.443 --> 00:19:48.130
This is my last point under Stalinism.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:51.540
The Soviet Union and
China were both committed

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:54.300
to the construction of
socialism within their borders

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:56.430
and never merged their economies.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.971
Their divergent national
interests inevitably conflicted,

00:19:59.971 --> 00:20:04.971
giving rise to rivalry, then
open split and armed conflict.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.110
The uneven economic development
of the two countries

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:12.890
and their starkly different
geopolitical circumstances

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.520
fueled the tensions between them.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:17.910
Situated behind the buffer
zone of Eastern Europe

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:20.070
and with a fairly stable industrial base,

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.790
the Soviet union followed a policy

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:23.720
of peaceful coexistence
with the United States

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:26.350
and established friendly
ties with autocrats.

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:30.220
When Suharto rose to power in 1965, '66,

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:32.065
as we'll see in a moment,
the Soviet union met

00:20:32.065 --> 00:20:35.319
with Adam Malik, his foreign minister,

00:20:35.319 --> 00:20:37.938
and arranged for the repayment of arms

00:20:37.938 --> 00:20:41.490
that the Soviet Union had
supplied to the Indonesian state

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:43.790
and that had been used
in suppressing the PKI.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.880
Relations with autocrats.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:48.940
That China in contrast found itself,

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:52.390
by the mid 1960s, threatened on all sides,

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:53.920
facing an eminent threat posed

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:56.980
by the U S invasion of Vietnam, 1965,

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:59.080
and the loss of its
largest international ally,

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:04.014
the Partai Komunis Indonesia,
the PKI, again, 1965.

00:21:04.014 --> 00:21:05.831
The Chinese Communist party sought

00:21:05.831 --> 00:21:09.380
to whip up armed struggle
throughout the region

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:12.060
to diffuse the threat of US Imperialism

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.950
to China's immense imperiled borders.

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.097
While the Soviet government
embraced Suharto,

00:21:17.097 --> 00:21:20.760
the CCP promoted Protracted People's War,

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:22.770
and armed uprising
throughout the countryside

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:24.510
of the world, backed by China,

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:26.740
the Yenang of world revolution.

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:30.950
Now this was not universally
the position of Chinese Maoism.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:34.880
This was put forward by
Lin Biao in 1964, '65,

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.700
became the policy of the
Chinese Communist party

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:40.865
in this period, and was
abandoned in the early 1970s

00:21:40.865 --> 00:21:43.610
as China reoriented and
established relations

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.920
with the United States.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:49.330
But for this brief
window, from '65 to '71,

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:52.370
in the Lin Biao phase of Maoism seen

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:57.370
from the periphery, we
have all of the hallmarks

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.243
of what became international Maoism.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:09.220
The PKP in the wake of the
defeat of the Huk Rebellion was,

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:13.370
at the beginning of the 1960s,
a dormant political party.

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:16.250
It had essentially disappeared

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:19.870
from the political landscape
as a result of this defeat.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:23.960
The re-emergence of the
PKP was a direct result

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.510
of connections established with Indonesia.

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:29.490
Here, the immensely influential PKI,

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.300
the largest Communist party outside

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.594
of the Communist bloc,
played a critical role

00:22:33.594 --> 00:22:35.540
in the dissemination of the ideas

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:38.300
of the Sino-Soviet split
throughout the region.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:40.030
And I substantiate this in great length

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:43.990
in the book manuscript that
I'm currently working on.

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:47.634
The ties with Indonesia, between Indonesia

00:22:47.634 --> 00:22:49.610
and the Philippines, and how this played

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.729
an instrumental role in the dispute

00:22:51.729 --> 00:22:54.520
that shaped island Southeast Asia known

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:59.070
as Konfrontasi in the
period of 1963 to 1965.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:02.480
The Philippines actually
was a core disputant,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.040
something that has been largely overlooked

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:05.950
by subsequent scholarship,
which has seen this

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:09.770
as a national dispute between
Malaysia and Indonesia,

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:12.310
over the formation of
Malaysia, it was in fact,

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:15.690
a dispute over the social
character of the entire region.

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:19.690
The PKI, through a graduate student in,

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:23.520
at the University of the
Philippines, named Bakri Ilyas,

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.810
oversaw the rebirth of the PKP,

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:27.660
bringing new blood into the party.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.630
Above all a graduate
student from the University

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:32.230
of the Philippines named Jose Maria Sison,

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:34.124
who will go on to become the head

00:23:34.124 --> 00:23:39.124
of the newly founded CPP
by the end of the 1960s.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.690
Sison was not immediately,
however, a dissenter.

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:44.350
In fact, he was the foremost
organizational force

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:47.140
within the PKP, instrumental
in their support

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:52.140
for Marcos in 1965, he was a
core figure in this, the PKP.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.098
The tensions that tore apart
the party were not yet present

00:23:54.098 --> 00:23:56.253
at the beginning of the 1960s.

00:23:58.110 --> 00:23:59.540
One of the things that's most interesting,

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:01.350
I think in studying the Sino-Soviet split

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.377
from the periphery is that the model

00:24:03.377 --> 00:24:06.564
of a sort of hubs and,
hub and spokes perspective

00:24:06.564 --> 00:24:09.960
in which Beijing and
Moscow are the two hubs,

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:13.380
and the spokes reached
from Beijing to Jakarta,

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:18.380
and from Beijing to Manila,
is only partially accurate.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:22.200
There was in fact a
complex mesh of relations

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.140
that ran from Jakarta to Manila,

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:29.140
from Havana to Manila, and from
Manila to Japan, et cetera.

00:24:30.722 --> 00:24:34.970
These, the dissemination of
the Sino-Soviet tensions spread

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:37.070
not only from the core to the periphery,

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:38.620
but throughout the periphery

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:40.563
in a somewhat autonomous fashion.

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:48.480
1965 was, not only for
the purposes of my study

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.857
of the Sino-Soviet split,
but for the purposes

00:24:50.857 --> 00:24:54.770
of world history, a global turning point,

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:56.604
I don't think this has
been adequately stressed

00:24:56.604 --> 00:24:59.860
in studies of the 20th century

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.140
where other turning points are stressed.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:05.308
1965, with the deployment
of US troops to Vietnam,

00:25:05.308 --> 00:25:08.190
in March, under operation Rolling Thunder

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:09.420
by the Johnson administration.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:10.480
By the end of the year,

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:13.605
more than 185,000 US forces
were deployed to Vietnam.

00:25:13.605 --> 00:25:15.830
And the crushing of the PKI,

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:17.260
the slaughter of hundreds of thousands,

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:20.130
possibly upwards of a million
members and supporters

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:25.130
of the PKI, in the rise to
power of General Suharto.

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:28.136
This fundamentally redefined the region

00:25:28.136 --> 00:25:31.794
and world politics, and
precipitated an acceleration

00:25:31.794 --> 00:25:34.541
in the development of global Maoism,

00:25:34.541 --> 00:25:37.481
the terms of the cultural
revolution shifted

00:25:37.481 --> 00:25:39.870
communist party allegiances.

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:41.870
The crushing of the PKI was instrumental

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:44.427
in the Japanese Communist
party shifting it's allegiance

00:25:44.427 --> 00:25:49.038
from Beijing to Moscow in 1966.

00:25:49.038 --> 00:25:51.296
It also set in motion the events

00:25:51.296 --> 00:25:55.840
that led to the Sino-Soviet
split in the Philippines.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:58.290
It was in the wake of 1965

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:00.591
that the Philippine
Communist party was compelled

00:26:00.591 --> 00:26:03.260
to seek out new international relations

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.210
that were not mediated through Jakarta.

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:14.453
In the same year, while these
events were transpiring,

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:18.500
it was a presidential
election in the Philippines.

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:21.290
Ferdinand Marcos had recently jumped ship

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:24.407
from his liberal party and
joined the nationalista party,

00:26:24.407 --> 00:26:26.510
and was running as their
presidential candidate

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:29.320
against the incumbent, Diosdado Macapagal.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:33.510
The critical question in
mobilizing mass support

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:36.270
behind Ferdinand Marcos was the question

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:37.593
of the war in Vietnam.

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:42.380
Johnson had secretly reached
out to Macapagal in 1964,

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:44.230
prior to the Gulf of Tonkin incident,

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:46.790
and said that he needed troops
to be deployed to Vietnam

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:49.570
for an upcoming surge in
US troops in the region.

00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:52.670
Now, he did the same with
Thailand, Korea, et cetera.

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:55.090
And Macapagal declared that he would

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:57.820
wholeheartedly send
Filipino troops to Vietnam.

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.398
This was going to be known as PHILCAG,

00:27:00.398 --> 00:27:04.040
the Philippines Civic Action Group.

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:06.210
That's a rather Orwellian euphemism

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:08.380
for the troops being deployed to Vietnam.

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:11.830
And Marcos in 1965 denounced
Macapagal for doing this,

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.730
claiming that Macapagal
was an aspiring dictator,

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:16.930
words that history would render ironic,

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:19.980
and that he was a puppet of US interests

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:22.401
and so on and so forth, and
announced that he was opposed

00:27:22.401 --> 00:27:25.210
to the deployment of
Filipino troops to Vietnam.

00:27:25.210 --> 00:27:27.790
And on this basis, the PKP, which had been

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:30.240
in an intimate alliance with Macapagal,

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:32.720
broke with Macapagal,
declared their support

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:35.240
for Marcos, and campaigned for him.

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:37.420
Central to this was Jose Maria Sison,

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:38.733
future head of the CPP.

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:42.150
Sison organized the support
from the youth wing,

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.523
the pakpak ng kabataanm,
for Marcos, a fact

00:27:44.523 --> 00:27:47.373
that he has attempted subsequently
to erase from history.

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:52.377
Within two weeks of the election,

00:27:52.377 --> 00:27:54.060
and an interview with Stanley Karnow

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.262
of the Washington post, Marcos announced,

00:27:56.262 --> 00:27:58.832
many of us felt that the
United States was preparing

00:27:58.832 --> 00:28:02.313
to withdraw from Vietnam, but now

00:28:02.313 --> 00:28:04.950
that the United States has
demonstrated this resolute will

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.580
to slug it out, we've been reassured.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.119
And he promised that he would
be deploying Filipino troops

00:28:10.119 --> 00:28:15.119
to Vietnam, reversing his
stance within two weeks.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.550
Interestingly, a side note,
but one can't help but note,

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:21.430
Marcos's campaign slogan, 1965, was

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:24.130
that he would quote, make
the Philippines great again.

00:28:26.871 --> 00:28:28.420
Right-wing populists are not

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:30.463
a particularly imaginative bunch.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:36.650
1967, a memo, this sort of
sets the tone for the split.

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:38.352
In a memo from Marshall Wright

00:28:38.352 --> 00:28:40.690
of the National Security Council,

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:43.400
to National Security Advisor, Walt Rostow,

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:45.770
he wrote, it would be nearly impossible

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:47.870
to overestimate the
gravity of the problems

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:50.870
with which our next Ambassador
to Manila must deal.

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:52.790
It has become commonplace
for people knowledgeable

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:56.840
on the Philippines to predict
a vast social upheaval

00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:59.828
in the near future,
there is widespread talk

00:28:59.828 --> 00:29:01.960
that the current president will be

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:05.960
the last popularly elected
Philippine Chief Executive.

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:08.510
There was one more election,
Marcos was reelected.

00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:10.000
But this was correct.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:11.930
He was the last popularly elected

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:14.313
Philippine Chief Executive until 1986.

00:29:15.310 --> 00:29:17.228
Many high-level American
officials considered

00:29:17.228 --> 00:29:19.120
the Philippines to be the most serious

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:22.180
and the most bleak threat
that we face in Asia.

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:23.610
That's a staggering statement.

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:24.550
Think of what that entails.

00:29:24.550 --> 00:29:27.600
1967, the peak of the cultural revolution

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:32.137
in China, Vietnam, the level
of sort of social instability

00:29:32.137 --> 00:29:35.663
in Asia, and the National
Security Council is saying

00:29:35.663 --> 00:29:39.830
the Philippines is, we got problems.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:43.549
So this is a period of
immense social upheaval.

00:29:43.549 --> 00:29:46.120
Shortly before Wright wrote this memo,

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:49.640
Marcos's forces opened fire
on peasant demonstrators.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:53.300
They were part of a millenarian cult armed

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:57.460
with amulets and bolos, not with guns,

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:59.913
and killed 33 of them,
in the streets of Manila.

00:30:01.182 --> 00:30:05.300
There were protests of
an explosive character.

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:06.657
The first of which took place in 66,

00:30:06.657 --> 00:30:08.960
as I'm about to deal with.

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:11.323
It was these social tensions that tore

00:30:11.323 --> 00:30:13.990
through the contradiction
at the heart of Stalinism,

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:18.050
at the core of the PKP, and
split it into two rival factions

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:20.833
along the fault lines of
the Sino-Soviet dispute.

00:30:22.210 --> 00:30:24.320
In other words, and I'll get back to this

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:28.162
in my conclusion, but
to preview, it was not

00:30:28.162 --> 00:30:31.930
either domestic disputes
or external machinations,

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:35.610
but rather the same heightened
global social tensions

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:37.687
that were accelerating
the Sino-Soviet dispute

00:30:37.687 --> 00:30:41.511
in the core, simultaneously
was producing social unrest

00:30:41.511 --> 00:30:46.511
in countries around the world,
Paris of '68, et cetera.

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:50.650
The rise of authoritarian
regimes, Suharto, et cetera,

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:52.840
This period of revolutionary upheaval

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:55.410
and authoritarianism tore
through communist parties

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:57.076
around the globe, splintering them

00:30:57.076 --> 00:31:00.130
along the fault lines of
the Sino-Soviet dispute.

00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:03.670
It is this shared geopolitical
and global context

00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:05.928
of class struggle and upheaval that gave

00:31:05.928 --> 00:31:09.483
the Sino-Soviet dispute its
international character.

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.720
The protests in the Philippines were,

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:21.010
over the course of 1966,

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:26.010
bound up with the question
of the war in Vietnam.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:28.050
Young people in the Philippines,

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.512
as young people around
the globe, were shocked

00:31:30.512 --> 00:31:34.930
by the televised brutality
of America's war,

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:37.549
by the hypocrisies of it, by the trampling

00:31:37.549 --> 00:31:41.580
under foot of the national sovereignty

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:43.080
of the Vietnamese people, et cetera.

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:46.040
All of these terms were being widely used

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:49.630
by young people in Berkeley, California,

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:53.583
and in Manila, Philippines,
and every country in between.

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:59.390
The PKP however had not only
supported Marcos in 1965,

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:01.800
they had quietly entered into his cabinet.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.450
Members of the central
committee were working

00:32:03.450 --> 00:32:05.460
in the offices of Juan Ponce Enrile,

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:07.457
who was the right hand man of Marcos.

00:32:07.457 --> 00:32:10.480
They were reluctant to break with Marcos,

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:11.960
but at the same time, they needed

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:14.860
to retain a hold over the youth
movement, their usefulness

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:18.180
to the Marcos administration rested

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:19.960
in their hold over a mass movement.

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:21.740
If they lost their hold
over the mass movement,

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:24.843
they lost their political value to Marcos.

00:32:24.843 --> 00:32:26.992
But to retain a hold
over the mass movement

00:32:26.992 --> 00:32:29.760
increasingly required speaking out

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:32.280
against the war in Vietnam,
which required speaking out

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:34.473
against Marcos's deployment of troops.

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:38.403
You're caught in an insoluble conundrum.

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:44.210
Over the course of 1966,
the party was silent

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:47.370
on the war in Vietnam and
its front organizations.

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:50.160
The pakpak ng kabataan, the
young people's organization,

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:53.313
in particular, the
nationalist youth, silent.

00:32:54.170 --> 00:32:56.120
They did not denounce Marcos's deployment

00:32:56.120 --> 00:32:59.493
of troops, not until October, 1966.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:05.009
It was in August, 1966,
that the battle lines

00:33:05.009 --> 00:33:08.165
for the future break began to be drawn

00:33:08.165 --> 00:33:12.690
along the geopolitical fault
lines of the Sino-Soviet split.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:16.870
In July of 1966, Sison, a leading member

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:19.870
of the PKP and head of the
youth organization traveled

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:23.520
to Japan for the 12th
Gensuikyo World Congress

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:25.590
on atomic and hydrogen bombs.

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:27.820
This is a fascinating historical moment

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:29.581
for the Sino-Soviet
split from the periphery

00:33:29.581 --> 00:33:31.800
in a great many countries,

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:33.341
because it was at this seemingly

00:33:33.341 --> 00:33:37.300
somewhat innocuous Congress,

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:41.340
a Congress, opposed to the
proliferation of nuclear weapons,

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:43.730
but that was connected
to front organizations

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:46.170
of the Japanese Communist party,

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:49.390
that a number of delegates
from a number of countries

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:52.820
throughout the global south
secretly entered China.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:57.434
It was in late July, 1966,
the dates are extraordinary.

00:33:57.434 --> 00:34:00.900
The Japanese Communist party,
as a result of the slaughter

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:03.850
of the PKI and what they
saw as the CCPs culpability

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:06.511
in this, because the CCP
had recently instructed

00:34:06.511 --> 00:34:08.550
the Japanese Communist party similarly

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:12.420
to engage in armed conflict,
broke with the CCP,

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:14.830
and reoriented toward Moscow refusing

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:18.440
to accept the Chinese Communist
delegation to this Congress.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:20.377
A majority of the
delegations to this Congress,

00:34:20.377 --> 00:34:23.052
including Joma Sison,
walked out in protest

00:34:23.052 --> 00:34:26.530
and then secretly traveled to Beijing.

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:30.940
They arrived in Beijing
on August 6th, 1966,

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:33.310
the day before Mao had issued his

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:36.920
Bombard the Headquarters
poster at Peking university.

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:38.340
It's an extraordinary moment.

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:40.700
It is the launching of
the cultural revolution.

00:34:40.700 --> 00:34:43.716
And for two weeks, as Mao
met with the Red Guard,

00:34:43.716 --> 00:34:47.168
all of the delegates of
the future representatives

00:34:47.168 --> 00:34:50.126
of the parties that would
split along the lines

00:34:50.126 --> 00:34:53.720
of the Sino-Soviet dispute were present.

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:56.906
They were hailed as the
revolutionary representatives

00:34:56.906 --> 00:34:59.070
of the proletariat and peasantry,

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:01.600
there was a gathering
staged by Chou En-Lai

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.474
on their behalf where
10,000 people gathered

00:35:03.474 --> 00:35:06.113
to celebrate them and
sang the East is Red.

00:35:06.949 --> 00:35:11.680
It was in this moment
that I think the character

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:14.290
of the Lin Biao phase of Maoism,

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:17.820
the Protracted People's War,
the Yenang of world revolution,

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:20.336
and the cultural
revolution was disseminated

00:35:20.336 --> 00:35:24.983
in the wake of the slaughter of the PKI.

00:35:24.983 --> 00:35:27.583
Joma Sison took these ideas
back to the Philippines.

00:35:29.630 --> 00:35:31.951
Events came to a head when
Lyndon Baines Johnson,

00:35:31.951 --> 00:35:36.951
October 24, 1966, traveled to
Manila for the Manila summit.

00:35:37.090 --> 00:35:39.747
The goal of this summit, which
was secretly orchestrated

00:35:39.747 --> 00:35:41.940
by Johnson, but presented as if it came

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:46.020
from Marcos, was in the words
of the Philippines, repressed.

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:48.380
The entire fire power of
the American delegation

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:49.750
during the summit was concentrated

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:52.321
on changing the complexion
of the war in Vietnam

00:35:52.321 --> 00:35:57.030
from an American war to a
war of, by, and for Asians.

00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:59.190
Entire thing was orchestrated by Johnson,

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:00.774
staged in the Manila hotel,

00:36:00.774 --> 00:36:03.600
the grand dame of American Colonialism,

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:06.130
the on the waters of Manila Bay.

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:09.670
And in a bizarre and macabre coincidence,

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:12.720
an American freighter collided
with a Philippines ship

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:14.780
the day that the Manila summit opened.

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:17.890
And I believe over 60 Filipinos drowned

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:19.710
and their bodies were
floating in the Manila Bay

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:21.760
during the staging of
the Manila Bay Summit,

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:25.203
outside the MacArthur
windows of Johnson's suite.

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:31.740
The youth organizing the
PKP, around Joma Sison,

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:34.920
where finally the muzzle was taken off,

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:36.540
and there was an explosion of protest

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:38.870
outside the Manila Bay,
outside the Manila Hotel

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:41.731
at the Manila Summit, and
it was brutally suppressed

00:36:41.731 --> 00:36:46.017
by the police on orders, both from Marcos

00:36:46.017 --> 00:36:48.193
and from plain clothes US officials.

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.151
In the wake of this, all
of the social tensions

00:36:53.151 --> 00:36:57.400
that had been percolating
beneath the surface emerged.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.440
The young people that were
the base of political support

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:05.440
for Joma Sison were looking
to continue to protest,

00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:07.760
no longer protesting the Manila summit,

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:11.760
but police brutality, and Sison, needing

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:14.080
to retain this as a base of
his own political support,

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:16.120
sought to corral these protests

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:18.150
behind the program of nationalism.

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.240
He delivered a speech at Ateneo University

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:22.930
on December 6th, 1966, in which he said,

00:37:22.930 --> 00:37:25.650
there is a need to wage a
nationalist education campaign.

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:27.480
The events before, during, and after

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.314
the October 24th incident, reveal to us

00:37:29.314 --> 00:37:31.969
how much our government
officials misunderstand

00:37:31.969 --> 00:37:34.050
the spirit of nationalism.

00:37:34.050 --> 00:37:36.364
Anti-nationalism has so
much poisoned the minds

00:37:36.364 --> 00:37:39.060
of so many of our police officers,

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:40.810
and those higher executive officials

00:37:40.810 --> 00:37:42.210
who give them the orders.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:43.950
Now I could go on in quoting the speech,

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:47.195
but Sison's point is,
isn't, he wasn't making

00:37:47.195 --> 00:37:49.173
the classic Marxist
point that the police are

00:37:49.173 --> 00:37:51.330
the armed bodies enforcing the state

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.650
which serves the interests
of the ruling class.

00:37:53.650 --> 00:37:56.110
He was saying, look,
they've been miseducated

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.680
by empty nationalism, and we need

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.725
a nationalist education
campaign so that they understand

00:37:59.725 --> 00:38:01.710
that they have a common set of interests

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:03.840
with the protesting youth.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.187
He was not, and this is the
critical point, instigating

00:38:06.187 --> 00:38:09.722
the leftward movement
of youth and workers.

00:38:09.722 --> 00:38:12.140
He was tail ending it, he was seeking

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:15.410
to constrain it as his
base of political support,

00:38:15.410 --> 00:38:17.744
but it was pulling him in a particular,

00:38:17.744 --> 00:38:20.803
and increasingly radical direction.

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.500
There were consequences as a result.

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:30.110
An investigation into the
police brutality staged

00:38:30.110 --> 00:38:32.720
in the Philippine legislature
very rapidly turned

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:35.110
into an investigation of
alleged Communist influence

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:38.150
among the protestors and singled
out was Jose Maria Sison.

00:38:38.150 --> 00:38:40.160
You can see him here
from the Manila Chronicle

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:42.938
where he faces prosecution.

00:38:42.938 --> 00:38:47.730
The, at the center of this
was the man in the black suit,

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:49.765
Carmelo Barbero, the man who rose to power

00:38:49.765 --> 00:38:52.599
as a smuggler, became a legislator,

00:38:52.599 --> 00:38:56.299
and was bound up with the
prosecuting of communists

00:38:56.299 --> 00:39:00.583
and alleged communists in a
series of witch hunts in 1966.

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:06.460
What's interesting, and I'll
get back to this in two slides,

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:09.350
what is interesting is
that Carmelo Barbero,

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:14.350
while he was the leading voice
attacking alleged communists,

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:15.940
and actual communists, said

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:18.510
to be a communist was a criminal offense.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:20.280
And to be a communist leader
was a capital offense.

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.270
At this point, the
communist party was outlawed

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:23.740
despite the fact that it had been allied

00:39:23.740 --> 00:39:25.341
with the former president
and was now allied

00:39:25.341 --> 00:39:28.030
with the current
president, there was a sort

00:39:28.030 --> 00:39:30.593
of universal hypocrisy in this game.

00:39:30.593 --> 00:39:34.436
Carmelo Barbero, the leading
witch hunter, was closely tied

00:39:34.436 --> 00:39:37.650
to the opening of ties
with the Soviet Union.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:41.930
His daughter studied at
Patrice Lumumba University.

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:43.554
Every visiting Soviet scholar that came

00:39:43.554 --> 00:39:45.280
to the country stayed in the home

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:48.713
of Carmelo Barbero, the leading
anticommunist witch hunter.

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:57.870
It emerged during these
investigations staged

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:01.470
in the legislature that a
large contingent of students

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:04.680
and professors were
scheduled to travel to China.

00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:07.060
Here you see three editorial cartoons,

00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:09.940
November 18, November 24, and December 10,

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:11.390
from the Manila Chronicle.

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:15.450
Red China is luring Filipino
students away as a pied Piper.

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.680
The Filipino student
is defying his mother,

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:19.660
the department of foreign affairs,

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:23.650
the defiance of mother is
particularly egregious,

00:40:23.650 --> 00:40:26.520
on behalf of the sneaky
Mao, hiding behind the door.

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.240
And finally, these students
are running rough shod

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:32.800
over the foreign office.

00:40:32.800 --> 00:40:35.010
This is actually rather far from the case.

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:37.021
They had been invited, their
visit had been approved

00:40:37.021 --> 00:40:39.713
by the Marcos administration prior

00:40:39.713 --> 00:40:42.100
to the protests of October 24.

00:40:42.100 --> 00:40:43.805
In the wake of the
protest, Marcos shut down

00:40:43.805 --> 00:40:45.970
the intended travel and declared

00:40:45.970 --> 00:40:49.480
that no one would be
allowed to travel to China,

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:53.923
not even tenured professors
at the various Universities.

00:40:55.480 --> 00:40:58.560
But while the Marcos
administration was shutting down

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:00.325
all travel to China, in particular

00:41:00.325 --> 00:41:02.010
because of the cultural revolution,

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:03.490
which is increasingly becoming,

00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:05.770
the world internationally was
increasingly becoming aware

00:41:05.770 --> 00:41:08.250
of the character of the
cultural revolution.

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:09.970
The Marcos administration was,

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:12.430
through other elements in the PKP,

00:41:12.430 --> 00:41:15.510
conducting informal diplomacy
with the Soviet Union.

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:17.340
This man here, Teodosio Lansang, is

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:18.940
a very interesting figure.

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:22.050
In the late 1940s, he traveled to China

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:23.670
where he was present for the founding

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:26.575
of the PRC in October, 1949.

00:41:26.575 --> 00:41:28.963
He was responsible for
Tagalog Language broadcasts

00:41:28.963 --> 00:41:31.410
at Radio Peking before he was moved

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:34.010
to Moscow, prior to the Sino-Soviet split.

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:35.740
He was in the Soviet Union over the course

00:41:35.740 --> 00:41:39.670
of the Sino-Soviet split in
the late 1950s, early 1960s.

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:41.159
His return to the Philippines,

00:41:41.159 --> 00:41:45.140
in January, 1967 was
orchestrated behind the scenes

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:46.880
by the Marcos administration as a means

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:50.680
of opening informal diplomacy with Moscow

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:52.660
to secure loans from the Soviet Union

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:55.001
and the Eastern Bloc, as well as a market

00:41:55.001 --> 00:41:58.930
for Philippine sugar and
other agricultural products.

00:41:58.930 --> 00:42:01.430
He was facilitated in
this by other figures,

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:03.831
William and Celia Pomeroy,
former heads of the PKP,

00:42:03.831 --> 00:42:07.670
now in exile in London, or in England,

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:10.310
and Rubin Torres, a figure
who was instrumental

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.270
in this and played a critical role

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:14.100
in the Marcos administration, a member

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:15.724
of the central committee
of the communist party,

00:42:15.724 --> 00:42:18.356
and also a salaried government official

00:42:18.356 --> 00:42:21.233
in the offices of Juan Ponce Enrile.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.562
The Marcos administration
had a loyal wing of the party

00:42:25.562 --> 00:42:29.100
that was looking to secure
ties with the Soviet Union,

00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:31.620
as Marcos was increasingly
making machinations

00:42:31.620 --> 00:42:32.783
toward military rule.

00:42:33.790 --> 00:42:35.877
In fact, the party, as I
demonstrated in my book,

00:42:35.877 --> 00:42:37.635
"Drama of Dictatorship," was instrumental

00:42:37.635 --> 00:42:39.683
in facilitating this.

00:42:41.390 --> 00:42:45.810
The Unified PKP, with
these wings torn apart

00:42:45.810 --> 00:42:50.810
by diverging social interests,
made one last united attempt

00:42:52.090 --> 00:42:57.090
at a political campaign
in February of 1967,

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:00.010
with the foundation of the Movement

00:43:00.010 --> 00:43:03.143
for the Advancement of Nationalism, MAN.

00:43:03.143 --> 00:43:05.660
The function of MAN, unlike, say,

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:08.580
the front organizations
of the party in labor,

00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:10.870
or in the peasants, or the youth,

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:12.757
the function of MAN was a relationship

00:43:12.757 --> 00:43:15.410
between the various front
organizations of the party

00:43:15.410 --> 00:43:18.540
in these sectors with
the national bourgeoisie

00:43:19.450 --> 00:43:21.230
with capitalist interests.

00:43:21.230 --> 00:43:24.010
This was the culmination
of all of those efforts.

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:26.900
And they worked tirelessly
to create this organization.

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:29.250
This is from the Program
of the Founding of MAN.

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:31.240
The names are small and
they wouldn't mean much

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:33.090
to most of the audience, but if you look

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:36.350
through them carefully,
it's a very interesting mix.

00:43:36.350 --> 00:43:39.023
It's senators, congressmen,

00:43:40.450 --> 00:43:43.090
neither tied to the
Communist party in no way,

00:43:43.090 --> 00:43:45.100
except by a loose alliance.

00:43:45.100 --> 00:43:46.920
It's what would be the central committee

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:49.580
of the future PKP, the Moscow wing.

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:52.470
Its central members,
founding members of the CPP,

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:54.778
the Beijing wing, and
its business interests,

00:43:54.778 --> 00:43:59.778
the head of a nationwide
chain of pawn shops.

00:44:01.001 --> 00:44:03.640
Workers tend to not be fond of pawn shops,

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:05.594
but there you are, pawn shops.

00:44:05.594 --> 00:44:09.490
And business interests
tied to banking, et cetera,

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:12.260
all of them merged together
in this organization.

00:44:12.260 --> 00:44:14.490
It was in the wake of the founding of MAN

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:16.283
that the party tore itself apart.

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:20.000
Both sides made a scramble to seize power.

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:23.940
The CPP failed, or the future CPP failed,

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:27.340
and was expelled in April, 1967.

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:29.520
They published a statement
in The Peking Review,

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:32.475
in June 9, written on
May 1, that declared,

00:44:32.475 --> 00:44:36.130
Mao Tse-Tung's Thought
Lights Up the Whole World,

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:38.040
committed to an uncompromising struggle

00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:39.570
against modern revisionism

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:42.850
with the Soviet revisionist
ruling clique at its center.

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:45.020
That was the founding
principle of the CPP,

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:47.100
opposition to modern revisionism.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:50.113
That is to say the line
of the Soviet Union.

00:44:52.550 --> 00:44:55.740
The critical question for
the now expelled members

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:57.347
around Jose Maria Sison,
and it wasn't simply Sison,

00:44:57.347 --> 00:45:00.160
there was a sizeable chunk of the party

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:02.230
that was expelled, in the founding

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:04.436
of a new party was the
formation of an alliance

00:45:04.436 --> 00:45:07.380
with a section of the ruling elite,

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:09.030
well, the section of the capitalist class.

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:10.730
This was intrinsic to their program,

00:45:10.730 --> 00:45:12.780
that bloc of four classes.

00:45:12.780 --> 00:45:15.820
This was what was the
critical element prior

00:45:15.820 --> 00:45:17.647
to the founding of the CPP.

00:45:17.647 --> 00:45:19.369
The PKP had an alliance with Marcos,

00:45:19.369 --> 00:45:21.780
Sison sought to retain ties with Marcos

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:24.040
over the space of about six
months after the expulsion,

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:26.000
writing a frenzied
letter to the president.

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:28.660
Again, and event that he
has attempted to bury.

00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:32.170
Eventually, however, the
party found new allies

00:45:32.170 --> 00:45:35.210
in particular, the figure
Ninoy Aquino, who even

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:37.164
for those outside Philippine
politics are probably familiar

00:45:37.164 --> 00:45:40.867
with the tensions between
a Aquino and Marcos.

00:45:40.867 --> 00:45:44.750
And it is here that we
see as another element

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:48.313
of the Sino-Soviet split,
and how rivalries aligned.

00:45:49.370 --> 00:45:52.096
While figures like
Marcos sought to use ties

00:45:52.096 --> 00:45:54.683
with the Soviet Union to
consolidate their hold on power

00:45:54.683 --> 00:45:56.700
as they moved towards authoritarian rule

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:59.733
at a time of social unrest,
figures like Aquino,

00:45:59.733 --> 00:46:03.326
on the other hand, were
the excluded opposition

00:46:03.326 --> 00:46:06.100
in a time of social unrest.

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:10.010
They too sought dictatorship,
Aquino was very explicit

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:11.680
that he would have imposed martial law

00:46:11.680 --> 00:46:13.860
if he succeeded in getting into power.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:18.520
The problem for Aquino was he
needed to be in power first.

00:46:18.520 --> 00:46:20.130
In my book, "The Drama of Dictatorship,"

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:21.160
I have referred to these layers

00:46:21.160 --> 00:46:23.360
as the conspiring understudies.

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:25.530
Marcos is our lead actor, but there are

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:27.710
the conspiring understudies
who are looking

00:46:27.710 --> 00:46:32.070
to remove him and play the lead
role of dictator themselves.

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:35.970
The line of Beijing at
this point, armed struggle

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:37.450
throughout the countryside of the world,

00:46:37.450 --> 00:46:40.260
but an alliance with a section
of the capitalist class,

00:46:40.260 --> 00:46:44.019
these two facets merged in the interests

00:46:44.019 --> 00:46:47.987
of these conspiring understudies,
the restive opposition

00:46:47.987 --> 00:46:52.550
among the capitalist class
in a time of social upheaval.

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:54.906
It is in this context that
Ninoy Aquino facilitates

00:46:54.906 --> 00:46:56.956
the founding of the Communist party

00:46:56.956 --> 00:46:59.320
and ties with the New People's Army

00:47:00.470 --> 00:47:04.680
from various former Huk guerrillas.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:06.408
The founding document of
the party declared armed

00:47:06.408 --> 00:47:08.840
with invincible Mao Tse-Tung thought,

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:10.984
the Communist party of the
Philippines will surely triumph

00:47:10.984 --> 00:47:13.740
and the Filipino people and the leadership

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:15.770
of the revolutionary
proletariat will achieve

00:47:15.770 --> 00:47:18.723
people's democracy first
and socialism next.

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:22.070
It's outside the sort of the confines

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:24.587
of this particular talk,
but my forthcoming book,

00:47:24.587 --> 00:47:27.569
"The Drama of Dictatorship"
deals with the next period,

00:47:27.569 --> 00:47:29.651
1969, the founding of the CPP,

00:47:29.651 --> 00:47:31.800
to the declaration of martial law,

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:35.100
and the machinations of
the PKP, the Moscow party,

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:38.099
which staged bombings on behalf
of the Marcos administration

00:47:38.099 --> 00:47:42.056
to justify martial law with the
complicity of the president,

00:47:42.056 --> 00:47:45.930
and wrote the documents
justifying military dictatorship.

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:48.700
And the CPP on the other
hand, which directed all

00:47:48.700 --> 00:47:52.030
of the unrest and protests
of the time behind Aquino.

00:47:52.030 --> 00:47:53.436
When martial law was imposed,

00:47:53.436 --> 00:47:56.137
over 60,000 people were
arrested for political reasons

00:47:56.137 --> 00:47:58.920
for the first five years
of the Marcos dictatorship.

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:00.847
The majority of these were not Communists.

00:48:00.847 --> 00:48:04.580
They were not members
of the political elite.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:06.613
They were ordinary workers and peasants.

00:48:08.570 --> 00:48:11.851
Mao, however, and this is my final slide.

00:48:11.851 --> 00:48:13.920
Mao, however, and the interests

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:15.080
of the Chinese Communist party,

00:48:15.080 --> 00:48:16.260
because of the heightened tensions

00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:18.040
of the Sino-Soviet
dispute, which had turned

00:48:18.040 --> 00:48:20.662
into an armed conflict
in the Xinjiang region,

00:48:20.662 --> 00:48:24.308
borderlands of China and the Soviet union,

00:48:24.308 --> 00:48:29.191
sought the support of Kissinger and Nixon

00:48:29.191 --> 00:48:34.191
as a counterweight to the
threat of the Soviet Union.

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:39.750
And reined in Lin Biao
altered the line of the party

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:42.950
from Protracted People's War
to the Three Worlds Theory

00:48:44.356 --> 00:48:46.510
in which the Soviet Union
and the United States were

00:48:46.510 --> 00:48:49.730
the two first world powers
and were the great enemy

00:48:49.730 --> 00:48:51.948
of the peoples of the world, and sought

00:48:51.948 --> 00:48:55.100
to form alliances of
his own with autocrats.

00:48:55.100 --> 00:48:57.740
This became the policy of the CCP.

00:48:57.740 --> 00:49:02.040
And thus with the rise
to power of Pinochet

00:49:02.040 --> 00:49:04.140
and the crushing of the
Chilean Communist party,

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:07.279
the Chinese Communist party
immediately welcomed Pinochet

00:49:07.279 --> 00:49:09.144
for the Chilean Communist
party had had ties

00:49:09.144 --> 00:49:11.660
with the Soviet Union.

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:16.020
In a similar fashion, when Marcos sought

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:20.227
to open ties with China in 1974,

00:49:20.227 --> 00:49:24.670
Mao welcomed Imelda
Marcos, September, 1974,

00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:27.714
you can see him here kissing
Imelda Marcos's hand.

00:49:27.714 --> 00:49:31.319
And Joma Sison, now head of
the CPP, wrote a statement

00:49:31.319 --> 00:49:34.350
in the Flagship Publication
of the Communist Party,

00:49:34.350 --> 00:49:36.707
heralding the visit of Imelda Marcos

00:49:36.707 --> 00:49:40.040
to the Chinese Communist
party, to Beijing,

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:42.762
as a diplomatic victory for
the people's Republic of China,

00:49:42.762 --> 00:49:46.093
a victory for the Philippine
Revolutionary struggle.

00:49:48.100 --> 00:49:50.687
In 1975, formal diplomatic
ties between the Philippines

00:49:50.687 --> 00:49:54.030
and China were opened, Mao
met with Ferdinand Marcos.

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:55.358
Marcos declared, while in China,

00:49:55.358 --> 00:49:58.630
that he would be crushing
dissidence in the Philippines.

00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:02.070
And no one in China made any
attempt to countermand him.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:04.940
They would not intervene
in these internal affairs.

00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:07.752
And thus the PKP was now in the cabinet

00:50:07.752 --> 00:50:12.603
of the martial law administration,
and the CPP was isolated.

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:16.020
The Sino-Soviet split had
split the Communist party

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:17.340
of the Philippines in two,

00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:19.600
in a period of increasing social unrest,

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:22.234
and now it was unmoored from Beijing,

00:50:22.234 --> 00:50:25.900
but it was set in motion
by the political line

00:50:25.900 --> 00:50:28.720
that was articulated from '65 to '70.

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:30.470
By looking at the Sino-Soviet split

00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:33.010
from the periphery, we
see a number of things.

00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:37.220
One, that it is a shared global conflict

00:50:37.220 --> 00:50:38.910
that is leading to these tensions,

00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:41.660
that brings about a set
of aligned rivalries.

00:50:41.660 --> 00:50:44.220
That this has disseminated, not
simply along hub and spokes,

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:47.800
but through a mesh of ties
throughout the periphery.

00:50:47.800 --> 00:50:49.023
It also calls us to sort of reevaluate

00:50:49.023 --> 00:50:52.920
the sort of bipolar
narrative of the Cold War.

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:56.770
Ferdinand Marcos was a
critical ally of Washington.

00:50:56.770 --> 00:51:00.922
And yet, here we see him
allied to a Communist party

00:51:00.922 --> 00:51:03.170
and conducting informal diplomacy

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:06.903
with the Soviet Union from
1965 through the 1970s.

00:51:08.152 --> 00:51:12.543
It indicates that, to an
extent, the critical question

00:51:12.543 --> 00:51:14.600
for us in understanding the development

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:17.530
of the Sino-Soviet split, and
the global Cold War, is not

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:19.350
to follow the map of geopolitics,

00:51:19.350 --> 00:51:21.060
but the map of the class struggle.

00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:22.798
For this, I think, is
the decisive question

00:51:22.798 --> 00:51:25.330
that underlies the Sino-Soviet dispute

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:28.540
and the tensions that it gave
rise to and that it expressed.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:29.373
Thank you.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:35.180
- Thank you very much, Joseph.

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:39.334
This is really a rich
and detailed discussion.

00:51:39.334 --> 00:51:44.334
A lot of, a lot of historical
episodes to explore.

00:51:44.910 --> 00:51:48.193
So I'm going to open it up
now to a question and answer.

00:51:49.330 --> 00:51:52.370
I'd like to give the priority
to any of our guests who are

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:54.343
in the Philippines right
now for the first questions,

00:51:54.343 --> 00:51:57.150
because I know that it's very late there.

00:51:57.150 --> 00:52:01.540
So if anyone has stayed up
this late to follow the talk,

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:03.550
I'd like to give you the first opportunity

00:52:03.550 --> 00:52:05.000
to ask a question.

00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:07.140
However, if you want to
wait, that's fine as well.

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:10.520
But the way we'll do this is you can use

00:52:10.520 --> 00:52:12.080
the raise your hand function,

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:17.080
and then I'll call on you to
unmute and show your face.

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:19.590
If you're having difficulty
using that, I noticed

00:52:19.590 --> 00:52:23.430
there's a lot of people
using the phone option today.

00:52:23.430 --> 00:52:26.200
If you have a question and
you can't get my attention,

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:28.650
wait until there's a gap in the speech,

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:32.520
and then just announce
that you have a question.

00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:35.670
But let's start with people
who may raise their hand first.

00:52:35.670 --> 00:52:38.120
Does anyone want to start
us with a question now?

00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:41.160
Joseph, while people are waiting

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:43.230
to formulate their thoughts, I'll start

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:45.660
with a question, a very naive question.

00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:49.151
I'm a Southeast Asianist,
but not a Philippine scholar.

00:52:49.151 --> 00:52:53.300
And I found your descriptions of Marcos

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:58.300
to be so interesting, you
know, because the way we,

00:52:58.420 --> 00:53:02.000
as non-experts imagine
Marcos, it really comes

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:05.480
as a kind of set of caricatures
in some sense, right?

00:53:05.480 --> 00:53:09.310
And so I wondered if you
could tell us a little bit

00:53:09.310 --> 00:53:12.350
about what your research
tells us about Marcos

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:17.320
and the way in which he
kind of moved effortlessly,

00:53:17.320 --> 00:53:20.020
it seems, between all of
these kinds of positions

00:53:20.020 --> 00:53:23.926
that seem to be polar opposites
to the rest of us, right?

00:53:23.926 --> 00:53:27.880
And one, what does that say
about Marcos as an individual,

00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:30.300
but also maybe how does
that reveal something

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:32.518
about these binaries that we often see

00:53:32.518 --> 00:53:36.840
as so, such polar
irreconcilable opposites,

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:39.790
but perhaps they're not
really such opposites

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:41.640
after all or something like that.

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:43.370
- Yes, it's a terrific question.

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:45.910
I mean, worthy of a talk in
its own right, I suppose.

00:53:45.910 --> 00:53:47.853
It's a common remark, but it's true.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:52.710
There, a very famous
Filipino author by the name

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:57.660
of Jose Pikakaba, writing
I believe in 1971,

00:53:57.660 --> 00:54:00.858
characterized the dispute in the elite

00:54:00.858 --> 00:54:05.640
in which one camp was
oriented around Marcos,

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:08.579
and then another around
Aquino and the opposition.

00:54:08.579 --> 00:54:12.470
And he said, if one did
have to give this a sort

00:54:12.470 --> 00:54:16.090
of class character, one
would see this as a divide

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:21.090
between the enchante and the
nouveau in the oligarchy.

00:54:21.550 --> 00:54:23.980
And the enchante is Aquino and company.

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:26.860
This is old established money,

00:54:26.860 --> 00:54:30.720
ties to Spanish colonialism,
American imperialism,

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:32.300
many of them leading collaborators

00:54:32.300 --> 00:54:34.110
during the Japanese occupation,

00:54:34.110 --> 00:54:37.030
large landed estates
and so on and so forth.

00:54:37.030 --> 00:54:39.760
The forces around Marcos,
and Marcos himself,

00:54:39.760 --> 00:54:41.900
and this is critical to
understanding the psychology

00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:46.461
from then, were the nouveau riche.

00:54:46.461 --> 00:54:49.560
They were the new industrial
interests and so on.

00:54:49.560 --> 00:54:51.950
This is not to say that they
were poorer by any means,

00:54:51.950 --> 00:54:53.511
and Marcos enriched
himself extraordinarily,

00:54:53.511 --> 00:54:58.362
but they were frowned upon
in the best of circumstances

00:54:58.362 --> 00:55:01.090
by the enchante, excluded, scorned.

00:55:01.090 --> 00:55:03.867
The enchante, and this is true
of this sort of social layer

00:55:03.867 --> 00:55:06.373
around the world,
consolidated its interests

00:55:06.373 --> 00:55:07.938
through incestuous intermarriage

00:55:07.938 --> 00:55:12.040
and sort of keeping it all
within the family, et cetera.

00:55:12.040 --> 00:55:14.890
There's a bit of a weed-eater
noise outside, I apologize.

00:55:16.210 --> 00:55:21.210
And Marcos resented
this and brought to bear

00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:26.350
in his sort of character, the quintessence

00:55:26.350 --> 00:55:29.250
of the nouveau, self construction.

00:55:29.250 --> 00:55:31.715
He was forever obsessed with the creation

00:55:31.715 --> 00:55:34.130
of a persona and he was brilliant at it,

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:36.856
he was cunning, conniving,
and absolutely brutal.

00:55:36.856 --> 00:55:40.662
One of the most famous early
stories from Marcos's career is

00:55:40.662 --> 00:55:43.940
that his father's political
rival was murdered in his home,

00:55:43.940 --> 00:55:46.290
shot through his head
while brushing his teeth.

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:48.706
And Marcos was convicted of the murder.

00:55:48.706 --> 00:55:51.959
And Marcos was put on sentence
subject to life imprisonment,

00:55:51.959 --> 00:55:55.023
and then was going to be
subject to the death penalty.

00:55:55.870 --> 00:55:57.850
But he was also, at the time, a graduate

00:55:57.850 --> 00:56:01.120
of the law school, and was taking the bar.

00:56:01.120 --> 00:56:05.010
And while in prison, and this
is a somewhat legendary story.

00:56:05.010 --> 00:56:08.070
While in prison, he aced the bar,

00:56:08.070 --> 00:56:10.870
getting the number one score
in the country that year.

00:56:10.870 --> 00:56:13.360
And wrote a three volume
appeal to the Supreme Court,

00:56:13.360 --> 00:56:15.630
asking them to throw out
the charges against him,

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:18.690
which was upheld, not so
much on legal grounds,

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:20.321
but because behind the
scenes, people said,

00:56:20.321 --> 00:56:23.430
it wouldn't look good to
have the number one lawyer

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:26.300
in the country subject
to the death penalty.

00:56:26.300 --> 00:56:29.160
And so a political deal was
reached and Marcus was released.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:32.190
This was his rise to national prominence.

00:56:32.190 --> 00:56:33.810
Deserved in some ways, but also,

00:56:33.810 --> 00:56:35.290
at the same time revealing of the level

00:56:35.290 --> 00:56:38.890
of brutality and Machiavellianism
that he would resort to.

00:56:38.890 --> 00:56:43.190
He then concocted a
persona by creating a story

00:56:43.190 --> 00:56:46.730
of having medals of honor
and so on and so forth.

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:50.230
He would turn on anyone who
was not immediately useful

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:52.770
to him, we see this again
and again and again.

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:54.180
And we see this not only domestically,

00:56:54.180 --> 00:56:57.860
but internationally, and
Marcos was extremely able,

00:56:57.860 --> 00:56:59.560
not simply at working
with the United States,

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:00.940
but working the United States.

00:57:00.940 --> 00:57:02.557
There's a wonderful book called,

00:57:02.557 --> 00:57:04.340
"Waltzing With a Dictator,"
by Raymond Bonner,

00:57:04.340 --> 00:57:06.270
that demonstrates how the Marcos's knew

00:57:06.270 --> 00:57:08.650
the ins and outs of American politics.

00:57:08.650 --> 00:57:11.580
They contributed a million dollars,

00:57:11.580 --> 00:57:13.480
stolen from the coffers
of the Philippine state,

00:57:13.480 --> 00:57:15.167
to the reelection of Richard Nixon.

00:57:15.167 --> 00:57:19.332
Amazing, huge contributor, more money went

00:57:19.332 --> 00:57:21.500
from the Philippines to
Richard Nixon's reelection

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:23.254
than went from the United
States to Marcos's.

00:57:23.254 --> 00:57:25.444
So there's that element,
and while playing that

00:57:25.444 --> 00:57:28.499
Marcos was able also to reach
out to the Soviet Union.

00:57:28.499 --> 00:57:31.561
I think that persona of self construction

00:57:31.561 --> 00:57:35.400
and that willingness to
do effectively anything

00:57:35.400 --> 00:57:37.766
in his political rise is
the critical character

00:57:37.766 --> 00:57:40.483
of Marcos and the class
interests he represented.

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:44.770
- Thank you, that was fascinating.

00:57:44.770 --> 00:57:48.110
I see there's a hand up
from Mathias Phoebeger,

00:57:48.110 --> 00:57:49.550
and if I've mispronounced your name,

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:51.493
please say it properly for me.

00:57:52.660 --> 00:57:55.661
- Sure, you got it almost
right, it's Phoebeger.

00:57:55.661 --> 00:57:58.020
Thanks, Joseph, for what I thought was

00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:00.700
a really fascinating
talk, it inspired a lot

00:58:00.700 --> 00:58:03.710
of questions from my end, but I,

00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:06.470
just to limit myself to one, I wonder

00:58:06.470 --> 00:58:08.683
if you could spend a little
bit more time explaining

00:58:08.683 --> 00:58:11.457
how the Philippine communist
movement interpreted

00:58:11.457 --> 00:58:13.542
the collapse, the crushing,

00:58:13.542 --> 00:58:15.880
of the Indonesian Communist movement,

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:17.510
because it seems to me
that it could play out

00:58:17.510 --> 00:58:20.100
in one of two directions.

00:58:20.100 --> 00:58:21.796
On the one hand, this crushing of the PKI

00:58:21.796 --> 00:58:26.540
may discredit the parliamentary
peaceful road to socialism

00:58:26.540 --> 00:58:28.840
that had been embraced by the PKI.

00:58:28.840 --> 00:58:31.535
But on the other hand, it
may discredit the allegiance

00:58:31.535 --> 00:58:36.306
with Beijing that had also
been embraced by the PKI.

00:58:36.306 --> 00:58:39.160
So I wonder if you could
just say a little bit

00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:41.100
about how that debate unfolded

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:43.690
within the Philippine communist movement,

00:58:43.690 --> 00:58:46.480
because this seems
fundamental to your argument

00:58:46.480 --> 00:58:49.573
and perhaps quite contingent as well.

00:58:51.240 --> 00:58:53.160
- That's a terrific question

00:58:53.160 --> 00:58:55.920
because it is such a critical question

00:58:55.920 --> 00:58:57.290
for every communist party.

00:58:57.290 --> 00:58:59.711
As I mentioned in passing, the
Japanese Communist party had

00:58:59.711 --> 00:59:01.540
to grapple with this question

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:03.290
and it precipitated the
shift in their allegiance.

00:59:03.290 --> 00:59:06.677
This was true for every
communist party around the globe.

00:59:06.677 --> 00:59:09.102
The events in Indonesia
were absolutely shocking

00:59:09.102 --> 00:59:12.123
and required an assessment.

00:59:13.689 --> 00:59:17.527
Internally, the discussions within the PKP

00:59:17.527 --> 00:59:22.527
likely polarized around
opposition to Beijing

00:59:22.680 --> 00:59:26.890
on the one hand, and the need
to follow the line of Moscow.

00:59:26.890 --> 00:59:29.137
So there was that, but publicly,

00:59:29.137 --> 00:59:32.340
it was written by Sison himself,

00:59:32.340 --> 00:59:34.880
who became the head of the Beijing party.

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:37.450
Sison, or the Beijing oriented party.

00:59:37.450 --> 00:59:41.010
Sison wrote a statement assessing
the events in Indonesia,

00:59:41.010 --> 00:59:42.950
which he published in
the mainstream press,

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:45.640
in the leading news
weekly of the Philippines,

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:48.523
the Philippines Free Press, in 1966.

00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:50.880
He had a sense of the scale of

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:52.910
what had occurred in Indonesia.

00:59:52.910 --> 00:59:55.253
He referred to nearly a million dead,

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:58.700
and then he wrote a political assessment.

00:59:58.700 --> 01:00:03.700
And his political assessment
was that this was the opening

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:06.670
of a revolutionary period in Indonesia,

01:00:06.670 --> 01:00:09.593
an extraordinary claim, historically seen.

01:00:10.780 --> 01:00:14.050
But one in keeping with the
sort of volunteerism of Maoism,

01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:17.807
you know, the paper tigers and whatnot,

01:00:17.807 --> 01:00:20.870
sort of it's all in the willpower.

01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:25.870
And his claim was that for
every dead Indonesian Communist,

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:30.470
there were four aggrieved
family members and friends

01:00:30.470 --> 01:00:35.470
who would rise up, and
that in taking power,

01:00:35.540 --> 01:00:40.430
Nasution and Suharto, had made
several critical mistakes.

01:00:40.430 --> 01:00:44.590
And among those, he
claimed they had alienated

01:00:44.590 --> 01:00:46.600
certain layers of the national bourgeoisie

01:00:46.600 --> 01:00:48.350
who had been tied to Sukarno.

01:00:48.350 --> 01:00:51.590
Again, that class, the
national bourgeoisie,

01:00:51.590 --> 01:00:54.350
that faction of the capitalist class.

01:00:54.350 --> 01:00:56.930
And as a result, these
layers would be driven

01:00:56.930 --> 01:00:58.023
to armed struggle.

01:00:59.300 --> 01:01:03.890
Sison genuinely thought in
1966, in the middle of 1966,

01:01:03.890 --> 01:01:07.210
with nearly a million
dead members of the PKI,

01:01:07.210 --> 01:01:09.840
that quote, the struggle for power

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:13.080
in Indonesia is far from over.

01:01:13.080 --> 01:01:15.550
One doesn't forget a line like that.

01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:16.824
The struggle for power
in Indonesia was over

01:01:16.824 --> 01:01:20.500
for a very long time, it
did not really reemerge

01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:23.610
until the 1990s one might
say, and when it did,

01:01:23.610 --> 01:01:24.780
there was not a communist party

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:26.331
on the landscape to speak of.

01:01:26.331 --> 01:01:29.119
Sison's argument, however, was in keeping

01:01:29.119 --> 01:01:32.320
with the perspective of the CPP,

01:01:32.320 --> 01:01:35.108
which he founded in
'68, '69, which claimed,

01:01:35.108 --> 01:01:38.333
martial law is good for revolution.

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:42.640
Fascist dictatorship
is good for revolution.

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:45.060
This remains the line of the party.

01:01:45.060 --> 01:01:47.796
I gave a lecture in March at Berkeley

01:01:47.796 --> 01:01:51.890
on the subject of a bombing
in the Philippines in 1971,

01:01:51.890 --> 01:01:54.160
analyzed the events and its significance.

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:56.940
And at the time I was denounced furiously,

01:01:56.940 --> 01:01:58.290
by the China, by the Communist party

01:01:58.290 --> 01:02:00.410
of the Philippines, and by Joma Sison.

01:02:00.410 --> 01:02:03.640
And Sison made a speech the
week that I gave my lecture.

01:02:03.640 --> 01:02:06.550
In which he said, and
I quote, the best thing

01:02:06.550 --> 01:02:08.360
that can happen for the
Philippine revolution is

01:02:08.360 --> 01:02:11.830
for Duterte to impose
a fascist dictatorship.

01:02:11.830 --> 01:02:14.070
He will be finished in a year or two.

01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:16.070
This, there's a continuity here,

01:02:16.070 --> 01:02:18.200
repression breeds resistance.

01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:20.900
And so the line of Sison
in the formative layers

01:02:20.900 --> 01:02:22.947
of the CPP was that the repression

01:02:22.947 --> 01:02:25.670
in Indonesia would foment and accelerate

01:02:25.670 --> 01:02:26.993
a revolutionary upheaval.

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:32.530
It's a fundamentally bankrupt
perspective in my opinion.

01:02:32.530 --> 01:02:34.477
And one that in some ways says

01:02:34.477 --> 01:02:37.090
that the best communist
is a dead communist.

01:02:37.090 --> 01:02:38.690
It's a terrifying perspective,

01:02:38.690 --> 01:02:40.600
but that was the response to the events

01:02:40.600 --> 01:02:42.543
in '65, '66 in the Philippines.

01:02:50.120 --> 01:02:53.203
- We have a question from Adrian Vergara.

01:02:55.990 --> 01:02:56.823
- [Adrian] Can you hear me?

01:02:56.823 --> 01:02:58.800
- Yes, we can hear you great.

01:02:58.800 --> 01:02:59.950
- [Adrian] Good afternoon to everyone.

01:02:59.950 --> 01:03:02.460
I'm actually from, I'm
actually a graduate student

01:03:02.460 --> 01:03:05.330
from the Philippines and I recently read

01:03:05.330 --> 01:03:09.540
the first Scalice's work,
early, earlier this year.

01:03:09.540 --> 01:03:11.130
It's his dissertation.

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:13.980
- Sorry, sorry to jump in, I'm
having trouble hearing you.

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:16.590
Is it possible to get a little
closer to the mic, perhaps?

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:18.865
- [Adrian] Okay, okay,
can you hear me now?

01:03:18.865 --> 01:03:20.300
- Yes, thank you.

01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:22.940
- [Adrian] Yep, so I
read your dissertation

01:03:22.940 --> 01:03:24.780
earlier this year.

01:03:24.780 --> 01:03:27.610
My question is less about the presentation

01:03:27.610 --> 01:03:29.100
and more about your scholarship

01:03:29.100 --> 01:03:32.403
on the Philippine Communist
movement as a whole,

01:03:33.440 --> 01:03:37.510
because recently, I'm sure
you're aware of Rigoberto Tiglau.

01:03:37.510 --> 01:03:41.209
I have seen him selectively
cite the research

01:03:41.209 --> 01:03:43.238
to push forward a certain narrative

01:03:43.238 --> 01:03:47.230
that justice, that justifies martial law.

01:03:47.230 --> 01:03:49.150
How do you feel about this as a scholar?

01:03:49.150 --> 01:03:53.584
And as a scholar, how
does one defend his work

01:03:53.584 --> 01:03:56.597
against, against the co-optation

01:03:56.597 --> 01:03:58.440
to promote certain narratives

01:03:58.440 --> 01:04:02.150
that justifies authoritarianism?

01:04:02.150 --> 01:04:02.983
- Yes.

01:04:04.260 --> 01:04:05.580
Thank you very much for that question.

01:04:05.580 --> 01:04:07.850
Let me, let me explain the
context of the question

01:04:07.850 --> 01:04:10.800
for the larger audience
and then respond to it.

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:12.528
I, but I appreciate this opportunity

01:04:12.528 --> 01:04:17.213
to present my own perspective,
as it's a critical question.

01:04:19.047 --> 01:04:23.260
History in any country
has profound resonances,

01:04:23.260 --> 01:04:25.120
but seldom is that quite as clear

01:04:25.120 --> 01:04:27.330
as in the Philippines around
the question of martial law,

01:04:27.330 --> 01:04:30.510
the question which will not go away.

01:04:30.510 --> 01:04:32.351
Particularly with the rise of Duterte.

01:04:32.351 --> 01:04:37.351
Now, a former leading member
of the communist party

01:04:37.790 --> 01:04:39.840
named Bobi Tiglau, he was the head

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:41.400
of the Manila Resolve Regional Council

01:04:41.400 --> 01:04:44.513
of the Communist party in the early 1970s.

01:04:45.620 --> 01:04:47.950
Subsequently broke with the party,

01:04:47.950 --> 01:04:52.040
became ambassador to Greece
under the Arroyo administration,

01:04:52.040 --> 01:04:55.410
and then a very prominent apologist

01:04:55.410 --> 01:04:56.694
for the Duterte administration

01:04:56.694 --> 01:04:59.245
on the front page of the Manila Times

01:04:59.245 --> 01:05:02.123
over the course of the
last four or five years.

01:05:03.516 --> 01:05:06.400
This another point that
I guess should be made

01:05:06.400 --> 01:05:07.872
for an international audience.

01:05:07.872 --> 01:05:11.174
The character of the Duterte
administration has been one

01:05:11.174 --> 01:05:15.770
of unbridled authoritarianism
and death squads.

01:05:15.770 --> 01:05:17.110
The estimates are hard to come by,

01:05:17.110 --> 01:05:20.596
but it seems likely that over
30,000 people have been killed

01:05:20.596 --> 01:05:23.215
by the death squads unleashed

01:05:23.215 --> 01:05:26.640
by the Duterte administration since 2016.

01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:30.290
A staggering figure, one that begins

01:05:30.290 --> 01:05:31.840
to raise questions of genocide.

01:05:32.700 --> 01:05:35.268
It's, it's an extraordinary figure.

01:05:35.268 --> 01:05:39.430
And Tiglau, drawing on
my doctoral dissertation,

01:05:39.430 --> 01:05:41.110
which he cited on the
front page of the Times

01:05:41.110 --> 01:05:44.484
in a series on the so-called
Scalice Revelations,

01:05:44.484 --> 01:05:49.484
cited my scholarship and
pointed out correctly

01:05:50.020 --> 01:05:52.033
that I had highlighted a relationship

01:05:52.033 --> 01:05:55.730
between Ninoy Aquino,
the opposition figure,

01:05:55.730 --> 01:05:57.300
and the Communist party.

01:05:57.300 --> 01:05:59.670
And then included, concluded,

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:01.860
fundamentally twisting my analysis,

01:06:01.860 --> 01:06:04.610
that martial law was therefore
warranted and justified.

01:06:05.640 --> 01:06:07.540
Something that I have
fundamentally opposed.

01:06:07.540 --> 01:06:08.900
Anyone who reads my scholarship knows

01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:10.830
that I respond with horror

01:06:10.830 --> 01:06:13.150
to the martial law administration.

01:06:13.150 --> 01:06:14.982
And that my scholarship is dedicated

01:06:14.982 --> 01:06:18.086
to a remarkable extent for a scholar

01:06:18.086 --> 01:06:19.590
to try and to use history as a means

01:06:19.590 --> 01:06:22.833
of preventing the
recurrence of such events.

01:06:23.777 --> 01:06:26.550
I have repeatedly spoken out against this

01:06:26.550 --> 01:06:29.970
on Facebook and so on, I
don't have a front page column

01:06:29.970 --> 01:06:31.360
in the middle of the Times with which

01:06:31.360 --> 01:06:34.980
to trumpet my perspective,
but I published a statement

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:36.624
that was widely circulated
just a couple of days ago

01:06:36.624 --> 01:06:39.610
because Tiglau was once
again coming to the fore

01:06:39.610 --> 01:06:42.757
because Bongbong Marcos,
Ferdinand Marcos Jr. has

01:06:42.757 --> 01:06:45.780
effectively announced his
candidacy for president

01:06:45.780 --> 01:06:50.250
of the Philippines and
is, terrifying to say,

01:06:50.250 --> 01:06:54.600
a viable candidate in the
sense of could get elected.

01:06:54.600 --> 01:06:59.120
And one of the key components
of the social media launch

01:06:59.120 --> 01:07:03.299
of his campaign was to misuse
my doctoral dissertation

01:07:03.299 --> 01:07:07.470
to claim that martial law was warranted.

01:07:07.470 --> 01:07:09.620
And no scholar wants to
be in such a position.

01:07:09.620 --> 01:07:11.510
My doctoral dissertation
has now been downloaded

01:07:11.510 --> 01:07:16.510
almost 15,000 times for
very, very strange reasons.

01:07:18.300 --> 01:07:21.210
So allow me to respond,
having created the context.

01:07:21.210 --> 01:07:26.210
I fundamentally and without
any reservation denounce

01:07:27.443 --> 01:07:32.140
the misuse of my scholarship
to in any way claim

01:07:32.140 --> 01:07:33.960
that martial law was justified.

01:07:33.960 --> 01:07:37.190
What Tiglau scandalously leaves out is

01:07:37.190 --> 01:07:40.110
that Marcos was himself
allied to a communist party.

01:07:40.110 --> 01:07:43.310
And that's a critical
element of my analysis.

01:07:43.310 --> 01:07:45.380
And then attempts to twist this

01:07:45.380 --> 01:07:48.130
into a justification for
military dictatorship.

01:07:48.130 --> 01:07:51.670
My entire argument is how
dictatorship can be avoided.

01:07:51.670 --> 01:07:55.360
And my point is that the two
rival communist parties allied

01:07:55.360 --> 01:07:57.670
with the rival contenders for the throne,

01:07:57.670 --> 01:08:00.120
subordinate the entire
movement and defense

01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:04.623
of democracy behind the
aspirants to dictatorship.

01:08:05.540 --> 01:08:08.308
It was this, this diffusal and betrayal

01:08:08.308 --> 01:08:11.390
of the movement for the defense

01:08:11.390 --> 01:08:13.788
of democracy behind anti-democratic forces

01:08:13.788 --> 01:08:16.533
that in the end made martial law possible.

01:08:17.770 --> 01:08:19.530
To oppose the threat of dictatorship,

01:08:19.530 --> 01:08:21.150
I would argue there is the need

01:08:21.150 --> 01:08:23.970
for the political independence of workers

01:08:23.970 --> 01:08:25.430
from every layer that's looking

01:08:25.430 --> 01:08:28.780
to coordinate some new
form of authoritarianism.

01:08:28.780 --> 01:08:30.161
And that is my response to Tiglau

01:08:30.161 --> 01:08:32.750
and the apologists for Marcos.

01:08:32.750 --> 01:08:34.850
And thanks for the chance to clarify that.

01:08:42.700 --> 01:08:45.360
- Thank you for that clear
response and the context,

01:08:45.360 --> 01:08:49.690
it's a fascinating and
clearly at the center

01:08:49.690 --> 01:08:53.750
of a lot of discussion in
the contemporary Philippines.

01:08:53.750 --> 01:08:57.913
Does anyone want to ask
any further questions?

